Falsifies `inter_process_communication::verification::receive_drains_a_pending_signal_first`.

The defect: the two arms are swapped, so a queued sender is collected before a counted signal.
It is the ordering a reader would choose on its own merits, because a synchronous message with a
thread blocked behind it looks more urgent than a count, and the function's own doc comment is
the only place saying otherwise.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(unsafe { e.receive(...) }, Receive::Signal)` when
`pending > 0` and a sender is queued. The signal is not dropped, but it waits behind a
rendezvous, which is the loss this ordering exists to prevent: a signal delivered with nobody
waiting must not be overtaken by a sender that arrived later.

